## Architecture rules

Every request goes through the same layers; each layer talks only to the next one:

```text
UI (page) → Pinia store → API client (web/src/api) → HTTP /api/* → route → service → repository → SQLite
```

- **Components** (`web/src/components`) are presentational: props in, events out, `v-model` via `defineModel`. They never use stores or the API.
- **Pages** (`web/src/pages`) and `App.vue` read store state and call store actions. They never call the API client or `fetch`.
- **Stores** (`web/src/stores`) own UI state and call the API client. Actions catch errors, expose them as state (`error`) and return a success flag.
- **API clients** (`web/src/api/<feature>.ts`) are thin typed wrappers around `http.ts`, the only module that calls `fetch` (ESLint enforces this). Their types mirror the server types: change both sides together.
- **Routes** (`<feature>.routes.ts`) declare URL, JSON Schemas (`<feature>.schemas.ts`) for params, body and responses, and status codes, then delegate to the service. No SQL, no business rules.
- **Services** (`<feature>.service.ts`) hold business rules and throw `HttpError` subclasses. No Fastify objects, no SQL.
- **Repositories** (`<feature>.repository.ts`) hold all SQL of a feature. Always prepared statements with `?` parameters (never interpolate values into SQL); map `snake_case` rows to `camelCase` domain objects.
- **Wiring** happens only in `server/src/app.ts`: repositories → services → route plugins with a `/api/...` prefix.
- **Errors:** every failed `/api` request returns `{ statusCode, error, message }`. 5xx details are logged, never sent to clients. Unknown `/api` routes return JSON 404; other GET routes return the SPA (production).

## Commands
{% if choices.docker == "full" %}
**Everything runs in Docker.** Node.js is not needed on the host and `npm` must not be run there: dependencies live in Docker volumes and contain Linux builds of native packages. Run every command below inside the dev container:

- `npm run dev` → `docker compose up dev` (API on {{ ports.APP_PORT }}, Vite on {{ ports.WEB_PORT }}, both published to the host);
- any other `npm …` → `docker compose run --rm dev npm …` (e.g. `docker compose run --rm dev npm run check`);
- `npm start` → `docker compose up --build app` (the production image).
